# faces/times.t88 - Times Roman, fitted to SPEC.md 6.4's 8-pixel grid
#
# Tinos is Steve Matteson's Times New Roman - the same widths, the same
# proportions, and free. It is the ONE LIGHT FACE in FONTS/ and that is
# deliberate: a book serif whose stems are drawn 2 pixels at a cap height of
# 8 is Times BOLD, which is a headline and not a page of text. SPEC.md 6.2's
# 2-pixel finding therefore does not apply to it, and the cost is real - on
# a CGA 640x200, where the pixel is 2.4:1 tall, this face reads thin.
# Charter and Noto are the sturdy serifs for that machine.
#
# SOURCE   Tinos.ttf
#          https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/fonts/main/ofl/tinos/Tinos-Regular.ttf
# LICENCE  SIL Open Font License 1.1 - see faces/LICENSES.txt
# SHA-256  60a0e8ef0c04dd5dd69ffe91025fa2ae5836cbd35600a82ba031977557e2cb61
# DRAFTED  python3 tools/os88ttf.py Tinos.ttf --name 'Times Roman' --psname Times-Roman --ppem 11 --rows 12 --ascent 9 --leading 2 --gap 1 -o faces/times.t88
#
# HAND-FIT  36 '$' - it overhangs the cap line, and the row clipped off
#           took the top of the S with it; redrawn on charter's shape
#
# THE ART IS THE SOURCE from here on. The line above reproduces
# the first draft, not this file: a glyph the 8-pixel grid could
# not take from the outline is hand-fitted here, which is how a
# bitmap face has always been made (SPEC.md 6.2).

face Times Roman
rows 12
ascent 9
leading 2
aspect 0

style regular
psname Times-Roman

